  "rules": [
    "Move every numbered payload from its own initial route region rightward to the common destination. The 8/12/16-arm cases contain 2/3/4 independent payload assemblies. Regions indicate progress, not prescribed grasp points or fixed robot pairs.",
    "Airborne transport needs at least two real beam grasps. Establish receiving support before sender release. Teams may change dynamically; the 16-arm half-ring contains 10 outer and 6 inner robots.",
    "After pickup keep the whole transported assembly clear of table/floor until final landing. The 600 g weight is suspended through physically closed interlinked eyes, not a cable. Grasp the red beam, not the weight, eye or shaft. L3 adds a passive spring-return beam hinge and a short rigid pendulum; maintain support while accounting for flexion, swing, release and future receiving grasps. No stabilizing robot attachments. L3 rods have different manufactured hinge directions: in_plane opens/closes within the neutral beam plane (local Z axis); out_of_plane bends perpendicular to that plane (local X axis). Per-payload metadata and bearing orientation identify the type. Axes move with the beam, not fixed world directions; no runtime mode switching.",
    "Drops are recorded. The last established sending team may recover; a receiver cannot retrieve a dropped object to bypass a handover. No reset or respawn.",
    "Only robot actuators and real contacts may affect the live task. No hand welds, object state writes, resets, hidden state tools or runtime physics edits."
  ],
